## Declaring what your medium is worth

A transport declares its settings in `OPTIONS` (see
[`../Architecture/transports.md`](../Architecture/transports.md)); the
coercion, the bounds and the rendering are written once for everybody. Two of
them are read by the **core** rather than by the transport, so a medium that
does not declare them simply does not take part in what they decide:

| option | what the core does with it |
|---|---|
| `priority` (−254…254) | how much this node prefers the medium when choosing between a peer's addresses, weighed against measured latency |
| `retry_interval` (s, 0 = off) | how often a known but unlinked node's addresses on this medium are re-dialled |
| `mlo` (bool) | may two links over this medium carry one peer's traffic together (**multi-link operation**) |

There is one more hook the core reads and never interprets, alongside
`endpoints()` and `stats()`:

```python
def idle_timeout(self) -> float | None:   # seconds of silence before this
                                          # medium gives up on the link
```

Two nodes negotiate how often they probe each other (`Docs/Architecture/transports.md`)
and can perfectly well agree on something slower than your medium will survive —
neither of them can see that, because it is a fact about the wire and not about
the pair. Report the number your `receive()` actually gives up at and the core
keeps the idle cadence to a third of it. Return `None` if silence never kills a
link on your medium (a spool directory fails on a write, not on a wait) and
nothing is taken back.

`mlo` deserves a word, because it is the one that costs something continuously.
Declaring it does not turn anything on — it offers the operator a checkbox — but
a bundled link is probed roughly every hundred milliseconds for as long as the
node is being used. Offer it where that is cheap (Ethernet, Wi-Fi) and leave it
undeclared where it is not: a medium that never declares the option can never be
bundled, and for a store-and-forward or radio link that is the right answer
rather than a gap.

